\section{Experiments}

We analyze 11 of ScienceWorld's 30 task types (245 episodes); the 19 excluded types lack actionable routing signal due to development contamination (5), taxonomic redundancy (7), or agent-floor performance (7). An anti-cherry-picking analysis confirms that death dominance holds in 18 of these 19 types, with a mean $\Delta\text{DR} = +38.2$\,pp exceeding the included tasks' $+28.7$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}).

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}

\section{Experiments}

We analyze 11 of ScienceWorld's 30 task types (245 episodes); the 19 excluded types lack actionable routing signal due to development contamination (5), taxonomic redundancy (7), or agent-floor performance (7). An anti-cherry-picking analysis confirms that death dominance holds in 18 of these 19 types, with a mean $\Delta\text{DR} = +38.2$\,pp exceeding the included tasks' $+28.7$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}).

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task scatter: death rate differential (internal $-$ deterministic) vs.\ performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). $r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$, $n = 11$ task types.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

Across 245 episodes, $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$; excluding death episodes, $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$, yielding DAF $= 90.4\%$ (95\% CI: $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$; at the default $P{=}100$). The penalty-independent death-asymmetry label fraction is 78.9\% (75 of 95 non-tie labels). Given observed $\delta = 0.265$ and $P = 100$, the predicted DAF $= \delta P / (\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}) = 26.5 / 29.37 \approx 90.3\%$, closely matching the observed value; DAF is thus a derived quantity once $\delta$, $P$, and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ are known, while the 78.9\% label fraction is the penalty-independent empirical finding. Of these 75, 54 (72.0\%) reduce to ties when the death penalty is removed entirely: in those episodes, both modalities score zero without death, so the only reason one strategy outscored the other was avoiding the $-100$ penalty. Death avoidance is not merely the dominant but often the \emph{sole} discriminating signal between modalities. The remaining 21 death-asymmetry labels involve episodes where the surviving strategy also earned positive score, but the death penalty still accounts for the majority of the score gap.

\paragraph{Per-task correlation structure.}
The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes versus 16.3\% for deterministic (2.6$\times$), consistent with the internal strategy's reliance on self-generated observations that compound prediction errors across steps. Per-task, death rate differential correlates with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ ($r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$; Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}). Since $P$ is constant across all task types, $\Delta_{\text{full}} = P \cdot \delta + \Delta_{\text{nd}}$ makes $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ a linear function of $\delta$ with residual $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$; when DAF $\rightarrow 1$, this residual vanishes and $r \rightarrow 1$ by construction. The near-perfect correlation is therefore a mathematical consequence of death dominance, not an independent empirical finding. We retain it as an internal consistency check. The correlation drops below significance at $P \leq 25$ (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}.

\paragraph{Signal concentration.}
The Signal Concentration Index (SCI $= 0.2147$; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}) confirms low concentration: the top-3 task types account for only $39.8\%$ of total death-rate differential. One outlier, \texttt{find-non-living-thing}, has zero deaths ($\Delta = +9.58$ from non-death scores alone); all 10 remaining types exhibit death-asymmetry-driven routing labels.

\paragraph{Sensitivity analysis.}
Episode-level bootstrap ($B = 10{,}000$) yields 95\% CI $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$ for DAF; per-task CIs confirm $\Delta > 0$ for 10 of 11 types. Cluster bootstrap ($K{=}11$, resampling task types) yields a wider $[73.3\%, 100\%]$; with $K < 40$ this should be interpreted cautiously~\citep{cameron-2008}. Both intervals exclude 50\%, confirming that death avoidance accounts for a majority of routing signal under resampling.

\subsection{Multi-Model Validation}
\label{sec:cross-model}

A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{llama3-2024} replication on 7 task types ($N{=}70$) shows the death-avoidance pattern reproduces and intensifies: internal death rate $34.3\%$ vs.\ deterministic $0\%$ (cf.\ Qwen: $42.9\%$ vs.\ $16.3\%$), with zero internal-preferred episodes (24 det-preferred, 46 no-preference). With $N{=}70$ and zero internal-preferred labels, this preliminary pilot demonstrates directional consistency of the death mechanism across model families, not full framework replication, which would require a larger sample and independent environment. Both models are in the 7--8B range; $10{\times}$ scaling results (\S\ref{sec:agent-improvement}) and Reflexion experiments confirm that death dominance persists across capability levels (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}).

\subsection{Agent Improvement Baselines}
\label{sec:agent-improvement}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
Condition & Death\% & $N$ \\
\midrule
Single-trial baseline & 42.9 & 245 \\
Retry ($3{\times}$) & 40.9 & 220 \\
Reflexion ($3{\times}$) & 31.4 & 220 \\
Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$ scale) & 34.5$^\dagger$ & 200 \\
Deterministic baseline & 16.3 & 245 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Internal-strategy death rates under agent improvement methods. $\dagger$\,72B death rate is the aggregate over 200 episodes across 10 death-prone tasks; \texttt{find-non-living-thing} excluded (0\% death at both scales). Reflexion partially reduces death but the gap to deterministic remains large.}
\label{tab:agent-improvement}
\end{table}

We test whether agent-level improvement reduces death dominance. Retry gives the agent three attempts per episode without self-correction; Reflexion~\citep{reflexion-2023} adds verbal self-reflection between trials (Table~\ref{tab:agent-improvement}). Retry yields only marginal reduction (40.9\% vs.\ 42.9\% baseline; the 2.0~pp difference is within sampling noise, Fisher exact $p > 0.6$). Reflexion reduces death to 31.4\% by rescuing 29.3\% of first-trial deaths (27 of 92), but the gap to deterministic (16.3\%) remains 15.1~pp. Trial~1 death rate under Reflexion (41.8\%) matches the single-trial baseline; the reduction comes from cross-trial self-correction, not from the Reflexion prompt itself. Trial~3 adds minimal rescue beyond Trial~2 (net $+4$). Scaling from 7B to Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$ parameters) moderately reduces aggregate death rate from ${\sim}$51\% to ${\sim}$35\% ($\Delta \approx -17$~pp) across death-prone tasks (Table~\ref{tab:agent-improvement}), but does not approach deterministic-level safety (16.3\%). Task-level variation ranges from $+5$~pp (chemistry-mix, where 72B performs slightly worse) to $-31$~pp (lifespan-longest-lived). This variation suggests that scaling yields large reductions in biological-reasoning tasks ($-15$ to $-31$~pp) but provides no benefit for chemical state tracking (chemistry-mix: $+5$~pp; Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}). The 31.4\% in Table~\ref{tab:agent-improvement} covers all 11 tasks; on the 10 death-prone tasks, Reflexion achieves ${\sim}$35\%, the figure reported in the Abstract. Recomputing DAF with 72B as the internal strategy ($N{=}220$) yields $90.9\%$ (95\% CI: $[83.9\%, 97.3\%]$), nearly identical to the 7B value ($90.4\%$); the death-asymmetry label fraction rises from $78.9\%$ (7B) to $84.3\%$ (72B). On death-prone tasks ($N{=}200$), DAF reaches $99.5\%$ ($[98.0\%, 100.2\%]$) with $95.9\%$ of labels driven by death asymmetry. Scaling lowers death rate but leaves death dominance intact.

\subsection{Cross-Domain Negative Contrast: MATH}
\label{sec:math-contrast}

On MATH Level~4--5~\citep{math-2021} ($N{=}795$ problems, code interpreter as deterministic modality), internal reasoning and code interpretation achieve near-identical accuracy: $73.5\%$ vs.\ $73.0\%$ ($+0.5$~pp, McNemar $p = 0.81$, 95\% CI $[-2.6, +3.6]$~pp). The label distribution is balanced (107 \texttt{int\_preferred} vs.\ 95 \texttt{det\_preferred}, 74.6\% \texttt{no\_preference}), ruling out routing signal ${\geq}3.6$~pp at $\alpha{=}0.05$. Without catastrophic failure, modality routing confers no advantage.

\subsection{Cross-Environment Contrasts}
\label{sec:cross-env}

We evaluate two additional environments lacking catastrophic death penalties. The ``deterministic'' modality differs by environment: an oracle simulator (ScienceWorld), a code interpreter (MATH), or admissible-action access (ALFWorld). These are not equivalent modality switches: each instantiates ``deterministic computation'' through a qualitatively different mechanism. Results across environments should therefore be interpreted as boundary probes of framework applicability rather than direct replications. The framework's diagnostic value lies in identifying different mechanisms (death avoidance in ScienceWorld, null signal in MATH, information asymmetry in ALFWorld) across heterogeneous settings (Figure~\ref{fig:signal-reversal}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/signal_reversal.pdf}
\caption{Cross-environment label distributions. ScienceWorld and ALFWorld both show deterministic preference, but through different mechanisms: death avoidance (ScienceWorld) vs.\ information asymmetry from admissible-action access (ALFWorld). MATH shows balanced labels, confirming no routing signal without catastrophic failure. ``Deterministic'' denotes qualitatively different modalities per environment (Table~\ref{tab:cross-env}).}
\label{fig:signal-reversal}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{ALFWorld.} ALFWorld~\citep{alfworld-2021} is a text-based household environment (6 task types, 135 games, max 50 steps). The deterministic strategy additionally receives admissible actions, an information asymmetry rather than a computation mode switch. The success rate gap is 27.4\% vs.\ 3.0\%, with 35 deterministic-preferred, 2 internal-preferred, and 98 no-preference labels (96 both-fail). The deterministic advantage stems from the information gap rather than death avoidance, a qualitatively different mechanism that the framework diagnoses without modification.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Environment & $N$ & Det\% & Int\% & NoPref\% & Death? \\
\midrule
ScienceWorld & 245 & 35.1 & 3.7 & 61.2 & Yes ($-100$) \\
MATH & 795 & 12.0 & 13.5 & 74.6 & No \\
ALFWorld & 135 & 25.9 & 1.5 & 72.6 & No\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Cross-environment label distribution. ``Deterministic'' denotes qualitatively different modalities: an oracle simulator providing ground-truth state (ScienceWorld), a code interpreter executing symbolic computation (MATH), or admissible-action access providing action-space pruning (ALFWorld). Only ScienceWorld satisfies all framework assumptions (paired modalities, binary catastrophic outcomes, sparse rewards); MATH and ALFWorld lack catastrophic outcomes and serve as boundary probes. $\dagger$\,ALFWorld's deterministic advantage stems from information asymmetry, not death avoidance.}
\label{tab:cross-env}
\end{table}

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router$^\dagger$ & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 tasks, 245 episodes). $\dagger$\,Single random seed (42) per episode pair; see Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness} for 8-seed robustness analysis.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points. The oracle improves only to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of decisions matching always-deterministic, an \emph{oracle degeneracy}: the optimal routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation.

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic Probe}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. The primary bound on performance is the oracle margin: only 2.89 points separate the oracle ($-6.58$) from always-deterministic ($-9.47$), capping routing upside regardless of router quality. A secondary confound is that ${\sim}27.7\%$ of step-level labels are misaligned with their episode-level assignment, further limiting step-level learnability. These two factors are logically independent: a narrow oracle margin limits routing value even with perfectly accurate labels, while mislabeling degrades learning even if the margin were large. In this setting, the oracle margin is the binding constraint: the 2.89-point ceiling means routing can recover at most modest gains regardless of label quality. An implicit router (Qwen3, LoRA fine-tuned; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) is trained at three scales (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) with identical 4-seed configurations. Step-level balanced accuracy is flat across scales ($56.4 \pm 3.5\%$, $58.2 \pm 4.0\%$, $59.2 \pm 3.5\%$; all within seed variance); step-level discrimination is not capacity-sensitive. At episode level, only 4B exceeds the always-deterministic baseline ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}), while 0.6B ($50.7 \pm 2.6\%$) and 1.7B ($43.1 \pm 1.0\%$) remain at or below chance. The 1.7B dip is robust across seeds (range $41.7$--$44.4\%$). We hypothesize that the 1.7B model has sufficient capacity to memorize death-correlated patterns in the training set but insufficient capacity to learn a generalizable routing policy, resulting in spurious anti-correlation; future work should examine per-class routing decisions to test this hypothesis. This non-monotonic pattern, combined with universally low internal recall, reinforces that learned routing does not scale predictably with capacity at current data scales. Unlike single-step tool-calling, the multi-step nature of this routing problem (10--50 actions with compounding state uncertainty) creates inherent label noise between step-level decisions and episode-level outcomes.

\paragraph{End-to-end deployment.} Deployed in the full ScienceWorld loop ($N{=}245$), the 4B router scores $-12.03$ (95\% CI: $[-17.82, -6.45]$), not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$; overlapping CIs). Paraphrase-based augmentation ($25\%$--$800\%$, synthetic oversampling without new environment interactions) provides no improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}); augmentation inflates the majority class without generating novel internal-preferred patterns (only 9 of 245 base episodes are internal-preferred), so real-data collection may be necessary but the $3.7\%$ base rate makes it expensive. The agent improvement results (\S\ref{sec:agent-improvement}) reinforce this ceiling: even Reflexion-level intervention leaves a 15.1~pp death gap to deterministic. Failure mode, augmentation, and ablation details in Appendices~\ref{app:failure-modes}--\ref{app:ablations}.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Why the router fails: task-type-level signal.}
The router diagnostic (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) reveals a structural mismatch: death avoidance is a task-type-level binary property (10 of 11 types carry death risk), but step-level features correlate weakly with this signal because death risk depends on multi-step state trajectories, not individual action semantics. The 4B router's episode-level improvement ($59.0\%$) without step-level gains ($56$--$59\%$, flat) confirms that capacity scaling captures task-type classification rather than generalizable routing features.

\paragraph{Non-triviality of death dominance.}
Three qualitatively different interventions all fail to close the gap to deterministic-level safety (16.3\%). Retry yields only marginal reduction (40.9\% vs.\ baseline 42.9\%, ${\sim}$2~pp across three trials; Fisher exact $p > 0.6$). $10{\times}$ capability scaling reduces death rate to ${\sim}$35\% ($\Delta \approx -17$~pp). Self-correction via Reflexion achieves a comparable reduction on death-prone tasks (${\sim}$35\%, $\Delta \approx -17$~pp). That two independent mechanisms (parameter scaling and verbal self-correction) converge to nearly identical death-rate floors suggests a shared bound set by the internal strategy's lack of ground-truth state access, not by any single intervention's limitations. Death dominance is robust under all three tested interventions. \texttt{find-non-living-thing} exhibits 0\% death across all conditions, confirming that death risk is task-specific. Compounding state estimation errors across multi-step trajectories create catastrophic failures that single-axis improvements attenuate but do not eliminate. Tree search with environment feedback and frontier models remain untested. Prior tool-routing work \citep{toolformer-2023,art-2023,chameleon-2023} operates in non-interactive settings where individual tool calls are independent; in multi-step interactive environments, the finding is not that death exists but that the death-rate differential ($\delta = 26.6$~pp) and its downstream effects resist all three tested intervention axes.

\paragraph{Scaling predictions.}
Stronger backbones lower death rates but do not eliminate death dominance: Qwen2.5-72B reduces aggregate death from ${\sim}$51\% to ${\sim}$35\% ($-17$~pp), yet DAF remains $90.9\%$ (vs.\ $90.4\%$ at 7B), and the gap to deterministic (16.3\%) remains ${\sim}$18~pp. The Llama pilot ($\delta = +34.3\%$) versus Qwen-7B ($\delta = +26.6\%$) is consistent in the capability-increase direction. Preliminary evidence (Qwen2.5-0.5B, $N{=}15$) suggests the relationship is non-monotonic: very weak models exhibit low death rates from inability to reach dangerous states. Our results suggest that in environments where one modality triggers irreversible catastrophic failures with severe penalties, death avoidance will dominate routing signal; self-correction mechanisms can selectively reduce death-driven signal but are unlikely to eliminate death dominance.

\paragraph{DAF and framework applicability.}
DAF increases monotonically with $P$ (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}); the penalty-independent label fraction (78.9\%) is the more informative primary diagnostic. The framework requires paired episodes under two modalities, binary catastrophic outcomes, and sparse rewards. ScienceWorld satisfies all three; MATH lacks catastrophic outcomes (negative control); ALFWorld lacks them too, and the framework identifies information asymmetry as the alternative mechanism. When $P$ is small relative to $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$, DAF degrades (at $P{=}0$, DAF $= 7.5\%$); the framework is most informative when catastrophic penalties dominate non-death score differences. NetHack~\citep{nethack-2020} and Crafter~\citep{crafter-2022}, both featuring permadeath, are the most direct replication targets.

